WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday, and as you can tell from the gray speckled walls behind me,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.100
in a stunning turn of events I have found myself

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:07.580
in an airport.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.600
So, Hank, since Brotherhood 2.0 started in 2007, I have flown more than-

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.100
[Airport announcement system in background:] May I have your attention please.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.100
Thank you for cooperating with the federal security required

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.940
[John:] Oh, no, it's my pleasure to cooperate with that federal security required.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.720
My only request is that two minutes from now,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.180
you let me know about the same requirement.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.800
So, Hank, since Brotherhood 2.0 started in 2007, I have flown more than 400,000 miles

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.700
and as a result I have a lot of perks.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.040
[Airport Announcer:] Smoking is prohibited

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.300
[John:] Oh, is smoking prohibited?

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.140
[Announcer]: at the Indianapolis International Airport
[John:] That's stunning. I would never have guessed it.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.040
Like, I don't have to take off my shoes when I go through security because I've been pre-cleared by the TSA,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.340
I get to board the plane first because I have a gold medallion . . . thing,

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.520
I can even go to the fancy airport lounge, although I don't, because it's extremely depressing in there.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.440
But, Hank, by far the most important perk is that when the plague comes that is going to devastate humanity,

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.300
I will be among it's first victims.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.300
'Cause that stuff never stars with people like, watching CSI at home. It starts in airports.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.560
And I've always said, if there's gonna be some human-ending apocalyptic event, I wanna be

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.800
right out in front of it. Like my zombie apocalypse plan? Become a zombie as quickly as possible.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.500
I don't wanna watch all the people I love slowly be overtaken by the undead.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.660
Also, I usually get to sit in first class. So, most people think that the people who sit in first class are, like, rich

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.840
people who payed a lot of money for their tickets, and sometimes that's true, but most people who sit in first

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.060
class get to sit up there because they fly with the same airline all the time and the airline wants to, like,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.000
thank them. Or, essentially, bribe them.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.900
So listen up, infrequent flyers. As you're walking past the first class

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.160
people to your, like, middle seat, second to last row of the airplane, I want you to remember something.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.200
You may think, "Man, those are the fancy people. Those are the people who've got it figured out."

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.900
But no, you, with your seat in the middle of the last row, you have it figured out!

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.180
Because, while it's true that I enjoy many

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.300
perks as a result of having spent more than 500 days in the last 6 years

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.300
in the company of the gray speckled walls,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.200
there are also some disadvantages.
Hank, I remember once I was flying to, like, Dayton, Ohio,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.200
and I sat next to a guy who had, like, not gold status or platinum status or diamond status,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.300
but like, diamond-platinum-elite-silver-plus status,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.740
like, he'd flown like 10 million miles, and I was so impressed, I was like, "Man, that is an awesome

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.400
status that you've got there!"

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.400
And he looked at me and he was like, "Yeah, y'know, it's a pretty amazing status, but

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.360
you know what it cost me? A marriage."

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.400
Now Hank I do realise that this is the first-worldliest of all first-world problems, like

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:19.400
"Wah wah, I have to go 30,000 feet into the air, and observe the majesty of Earth on a regular basis."

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.800
The thing about travelling all the time is, that while it's true that you get to be the first person to die

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.860
in like the rabbit plague or whatever, and that's huge, you also have to spend a lot of time away from your family.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.500
And that sucks, which is why Hank I am so excited that this is my last trip to the gray speckled walls

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.500
until August when I go to VidCon.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.700
Hank, I cannot tell you how excited I am to become an infrequent flyer.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.800
I am going to make lots of videos and I am going to work on my new book.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.500
But most importantly I am going to hang out with my family.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.880
Which hopefully includes you and the Katherine, Hank.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.840
But you've got to come her because as previously noted. I am not flying!

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.400
Hank, in the comments of my video from last week where I cooked dinner with Henry,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.500
a lot of people were like, "You're such a great Dad!"

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.200
and I hope that I am a good Dad. 
But if I am, it's not because I can like make some jokes

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.400
while holding a video camera and overcooking asparagus.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.020
Hank when I was back at my child-
[Airport Announcer:] Ladies and Gentlemen of  (?) Airlines paging...

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.640
Why do people always have to get paged? I have never been paged.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:12.360
I have spent 500 days in airports in the last six years and never once, have I needed to be paged.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.000
Schermg, the light is changing.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.040
I have to move because the risen Sun is too bright in my woozing eyes.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.940
Ultimately Hank I think a big part of being a parent is being around.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:27.540
So I'm going to try that out for a while. Why did- What is this? Wha- Why did no-one tell me about this?

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.920
I walked through the airport. What? How did this happen?

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.460
Has this been going on the entire video?

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.500
Great, now the people in the airport think that I am extra crazy

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.800
because in addition to holding a video camera in front of myself,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.800
I've been half naked the entire time!

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.640
Oh my god, I have to go hide somewhere. 
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

